From: kevin03L <kevin03L@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: tegra210: program PLLM again after a suspend

After a suspend of a Jetson Nano the memory was 18 % slower, for good: 15.4 GB/s seen from the GPU
instead of 18.6, and 3D with it. The warm boot firmware turns PLLM on at a rate of its own (M=1,
N=34: 1305.6 MHz) while the memory runs from PLLP at 204 MHz. clk_restore_context() does not touch
a PLL that is on, the clock framework believes PLLM is still at 1600 MHz, and the memory is switched
to it.

Turn PLLM off when the memory does not use it, so that it is programmed again. Measured on a
reComputer J1020 v2: 18.6 GB/s after each of 9 suspends.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/drivers/clk/tegra/clk-tegra210.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/tegra/clk-tegra210.c
@@ -3468,6 +3468,7 @@
 static void tegra210_clk_resume(void *data)
 {
 	unsigned int i;
+	u32 value;
 
 	tegra_clk_osc_resume(clk_base);
 
@@ -3500,6 +3501,22 @@
 	/* wait for all writes to happen to have all the clocks enabled */
 	fence_udelay(2, clk_base);
 
+	/*
+	 * The boot firmware turns PLLM on at a rate of its own (1305.6 MHz on
+	 * a Jetson Nano), and clk_restore_context() leaves a PLL that is on
+	 * alone: the memory would later run from it at that rate instead of
+	 * 1600 MHz. The firmware runs the memory from PLLP: turn PLLM off, so
+	 * that it is programmed again.
+	 */
+	value = readl_relaxed(clk_base + CLK_SOURCE_EMC) >> 29;
+	if (value != 0 && value != 4) { /* PLLM, PLLM_UD */
+		value = readl_relaxed(clk_base + PLLM_BASE);
+		writel_relaxed(value & ~PLL_ENABLE, clk_base + PLLM_BASE);
+		fence_udelay(1, clk_base);
+	} else {
+		pr_warn("tegra210: memory resumed from PLLM\n");
+	}
+
 	/* restore PLLs and all peripheral clock rates */
 	tegra210_init_pllu();
 	clk_restore_context();
